     "text": "When some person asks the bank for a loan and the bank, after taking security etc., becomes ready to give the loan, it does not give that person cash rupees; rather it merely enters in its account books, in his name, notionally that “this much money of yours has been deposited with the bank as a trust”, and giving him a cheque book directs him that he should not withdraw cash now, but rather give a cheque of the particular sum to the one whom he has to pay. The one receiving the cheque deposits this cheque in his account in the same bank. As it were, that cheque comes back again to the same bank, and the bank does not need to give cash rupees. This is called creating money. Because the bank, instead of issuing cash rupees, created a notional “deposit” with itself which, for the customer, is exactly equal to cash rupees. The creation of this deposit is “creation of money”.",
