   "content": "<p>Credit Creation</p><p>When some person asks the bank for a loan and the bank, after taking security etc., becomes ready to give the loan, it does not give that person cash rupees; rather it merely enters in its account books, in his name, notionally that “this much money of yours has been deposited with the bank as a trust”, and giving him a cheque book directs him that he should not withdraw cash now, but rather give a cheque of the particular sum to the one whom he has to pay. The one receiving the cheque deposits this cheque in his account in the same bank. As it were, that cheque comes back again to the same bank, and the bank does not need to give cash rupees. This is called creating money. Because the bank, instead of issuing cash rupees, created a notional “deposit” with itself which, for the customer, is exactly equal to cash rupees. The creation of this deposit is “creation of money”.</p><p>How is credit created? (How Credit is Created)</p><p>It was stated above that commercial banks, by giving loans, create money through the expansion of credit money. Here we discuss this problem how banks can create money and how much money. For its explanation we assume three things.</p><p>Assumptions</p><p>i. The first thing is that every bank is legally bound to keep twenty-five per cent of its total liabilities with it in the form of cash. Liabilities mean those sums which are payable by the bank, i.e. the deposits of people etc.</p><p>ii. The second assumption is that all payments and receipts take place through cheques, not in cash form.</p><p>iii. The third assumption is that in some one bank some person deposits a new deposit of four hundred rupees.</p><p>First we only see how and how much money one bank creates, and then we will see in what quantity all banks together in the whole country can create money collectively.</p><p>Credit creation by a single bank (Credit Creation by Single Bank)</p><p>Suppose that a person deposits four hundred rupees as a deposit with some bank. From this, on one side the assets or receivable sums of the bank will increase by four hundred rupees. Assets mean those sums which are the property of the bank; and on the other side its liabilities, i.e. payable sums, will increase by four hundred rupees, because ultimately this deposited sum will have to be paid back to the depositor. The bank will not be able to keep this sum with it in cash form, because it is legally bound to keep only twenty-five per cent in cash. Therefore, keeping one hundred rupees cash, it will lend the remaining three hundred rupees on interest to a needy trader. Its balance sheet (Balance Sheet) will take the following form.</p><p>Of the 400 rupees deposited with the bank, the bank has issued 300 rupees as a loan. This sum will be deposited into the account of the borrower … (continued on p.51)</p>",
