   "content": "<p>Specimen order cheque</p><p>3. Crossed or marked cheque (Crossed Cheque)</p><p>By this is meant such a cheque on whose upper corner two parallel lines are drawn and between them “and Company” (“and Co”) is written in this way. The payment of a cheque of this kind is not made directly in the form of cash; rather the one receiving the cheque can deposit its sum only in his own account, and afterwards withdraw it through a bearer or identity cheque. If the cheque is intended to be given only to that person whose name has been written, then on the cheque these words are written: (Payee’s Account Only) (i.e. deposit only in the account of the receiver).</p><p>Specimen crossed cheque</p><p>4. Traveller’s Cheque</p><p>By this is meant such a cheque which is prepared for a traveller going from one city to another city or from one country to another country. For example, a person of Pakistan undertakes the journey of another country for sightseeing or for a business purpose; then he deposits the sum of his needs here in some bank, and gets made a travel cheque book consisting of small and large values of some foreign currency. While issuing the cheques the bank gets this person to sign every cheque. When this person reaches the foreign country, there, according to need, he presents a small or large cheque in a bank and receives the cash. The foreign bank, before giving cash in exchange for the cheque, gets the said person to sign the same cheque, so that it may satisfy itself that this signature exactly matches the signatures recorded before, and that really this same person is the actual owner of the cheque. (The Government of Pakistan usually arranges to give traveller’s cheques to intending pilgrims of Hajj.) Within the country too, traveller’s cheques are prepared for carrying sums from one city to another. The bank collects a fee for making traveller’s cheques.</p><p>5. Bank Draft</p><p>A bank draft is used for sending money from one place to another. Suppose Arif wants to send one lakh rupees from Lahore to his brother Asif in Karachi. Its method is that he deposits this money with some bank in Lahore. The bank will write an order in the name of its branch in Karachi … (continued on p.56)</p>",
