   "content": "<p>(continued from p.76) … tax, spend this sum on providing such social and public services from which the poor class benefits, e.g. facilities of education and health, housing construction; [thus] it can fulfil the requirements of a welfare state.</p><p>3. Effects on production and consumption</p><p>The government (i.e. fiscal) policy regarding government income and government expenditure also affects the production and consumption of goods in the country; because the government, to those industries which it wants to give protection, or whose production it wants to encourage, or whose manufactures it wants to export more, gives protection (Protection) from competition with foreign (countries’ production); regarding them it adopts a soft policy of taxes, or for some period provides them exemption from taxes. As it were, by changing the direction of taxes and the policy, the productive and economic activities of the country can be influenced according to need.</p><p>4. Acceleration in the rate of growth (Growth Rate)</p><p>In backward countries the importance of public finance is even greater, because with the help of fiscal policy the speed of increasing economic development, capital formation, national income and employment opportunities can be made faster.</p><p>5. Allocation of resources (Allocation)</p><p>Through the government’s financial and budget (Budget) policy, the allocation of the economic resources of the country can be made, according to need, into different uses: on the production of which goods, how much economic and financial resources should be used.</p>",
