   "content": "<p>1. Obtaining foreign goods (Availability of Foreign Goods)</p><p>A country can import from another country such things which it cannot make itself, or for whose making it does not have the necessary resources, e.g. many countries of the world do not make cotton yarn themselves, or cannot make it; therefore they import it from Pakistan. Pakistan cannot itself make aircraft; therefore it imports them from other countries.</p><p>2. Obtaining difficult products (Availability of difficult goods)</p><p>By virtue of international trade a country can obtain those things whose making is difficult for it, e.g. Pakistan cannot easily make some kinds of machines and chemicals; therefore these are imported from other countries.</p><p>3. Benefits of specialisation and division of labour (Advantages of Specialization)</p><p>By virtue of international trade every country employs its “productive resources” (Productive Resources) in making those goods which it can make at low cost. In this way every country takes benefit from the process of specialisation (Division of Labour Specialization). For example, we have invested most of our capital in the cloth industry, because cotton is produced more here, and we export cotton cloth and import machines from Europe, because there the price of machines is lower.</p><p>4. Availability of goods in times of shortage (Shortage)</p><p>Because of international trade every country becomes able to obtain necessities of life from outside in emergency or bad conditions, e.g. in days of famine and calamities; as Pakistan, in periods of drought and shortage of food, imports wheat from America, and from India potatoes, onions, sugar etc.</p><p>5. Stability in prices (Stability in Pakistan)</p><p>By international trade breadth arises in supply and demand, the scope of buying and selling spreads, and the rise and fall of prices becomes less. For example, if we do not export cotton, its price here will fall very much and farmers will suffer loss, whereas in other countries the prices of cotton may be touching the sky. Therefore, because of export, on one side Pakistani farmers get a reasonable price for their produce, and on the other side no great difference remains in the prices of cotton in Pakistan and the importing countries.</p><p>6. Promotion of knowledge and culture</p><p>By virtue of international trade a deep link and relationship arises among different countries, and besides obtaining trade benefits, they exchange ideas and theories for cultural and civilisational progress too; e.g. we are influenced by the sciences and arts, technology and developmental way of thinking of America, England, Japan and China (China).</p><p>7. Disposal of surplus output (Disposal of Surplus Output)</p><p>By virtue of international trade a country can obtain wealth by selling its surplus produce (which is left over from its need) to other countries. If there were no custom of taking merchandise to foreign markets, the surplus produce of every country would be wasted.</p><p>8. Benefits of the law of increasing returns (Benefits of Increasing return to scale)</p><p>Because of international trade markets become wide; goods are produced on a large scale, and in this way the benefits of the law of increasing returns of production … (continued on p.85)</p>",
