   "content": "<p>(continued from p.85) … keep making plans to wage war against them, as in September 2003 America’s attack on Iraq.</p><p>4. Unbalanced growth (Unbalanced Growth)</p><p>Since, because of international trade, a country obtains specialisation (Specialization) in the production of some particular thing, therefore it pays no attention to other industries. The result is that some useful industries in the country cannot develop. In Pakistan special attention has been paid to the cloth industry, and heavy engineering and the iron and steel industry are being ignored, although its establishment is very necessary for economic development.</p><p>5. Susceptibility to international economic conditions (Influence of international ewnomic situation)</p><p>Because of international trade a deep link is established among different countries; in economic terms they become dependent on one another. If economic disturbance arises in some country, e.g. slump (Slump), or over-production (Over-Production) beyond need, or economic crisis (Economics Crisis) etc., then its effect falls on the economic life of all other countries. The world depression of 1929 is a clear example. Its beginning was from America, but it took the whole world into its grip. In the same way, under the Second World War (1939 to 1945), world trade was affected. In 1974 with the increase in the price of oil, and in October 2008 because of America’s economic crisis, the economies of all countries were affected.</p><p>6. Shortage of raw material</p><p>The country which exports its raw material to other countries, its industrialists remain in great loss, because foreigners, giving a higher price, buy the raw material, and for domestic industrialists raw material is not left. The result is that industry cannot develop there.</p><p>7. Danger to freedom and sovereignty (Danger to Sovereignty)</p><p>Foreign trade sometimes causes severe harm to political freedom. Many weak nations became slaves for this very reason. You know that in the Pakistan-India subcontinent the English first came for doing trade, but later they became masters of it.</p><p>From the above discussion it becomes known that international trade has benefits as well as harms. But in spite of these harms it is impossible for any nation to remain separate from international trade. No nation can remain self-sufficient; therefore effort should be made that, as far as possible, the harms of international trade be ended and its benefits be obtained.</p><p>Classical theory of international trade (Classical Theory)</p><p>The explanation of the basis of international trade was first given by the classical economist David [Ricardo], and in this connection he presented the theory of comparative costs (Comparative Costs). He told that since within one country labourers keep moving from one profession to another profession and from one industry to another industry, therefore the rate of wages in all industries becomes the same. But since generally the labourers of one country do not migrate (Migrate) to another country (i.e. international mobility (Mobility of Labour) does not exist among labourers), therefore the rate of their wages among different countries does not become the same, and the result is that among countries … (continued on p.87)</p>",
