   "content": "<p>(continued from p.168) … the public gets encouragement to save money. All utility bills are received. According to the standard of the Universal Postal Union (UPU) there should be one post office for 20 to 40 square kilometres and for a population of three to six thousand. According to its rules Pakistan exchanges post with all countries of the world (except Israel). Last year Pakistan Post earned 40 crore rupees in the form of commission.</p><p>Telegraph (Telegraph)</p><p>At the time of the establishment of Pakistan, telegram facilities too were insufficient here. But later teleprinter exchanges have been installed in Karachi, Lahore and Rawalpindi. Karachi has been linked through an international radio-telegraph circuit with these cities: Tehran, Moscow, Paris, London, New York, Amsterdam, Osaka, Hamburg and Jeddah.</p><p>Pakistan Telecommunication Authority (PTA)</p><p>At the beginning of 2011, in the country the number of fixed-type (Fixed Line) telephone connections, decreasing continuously, became 34 lakh, and the total number of mobile phone consumers, increasing, became ten crore twenty lakh. As it were, 19 per cent of the total population had the facility of a phone. To regulate this sector an institution, Pakistan Telecom Authority (PTA), was established in January 1997. It makes the establishment and operation of the telecommunication system regular and regulated and protects the interests of consumers, so that good service is provided to them at economical cost.</p><p>The government adopted the policy of privatisation in this sector, and gave PTA the authority to issue licences for fixed (fixed line) and mobile phones. For the progress of this sector the government has given many concessions and has made reductions in taxes and duties.</p><p>Now telecommunication has become a basic need for the progress and renewal of the different sectors of the economy of any country, because as an economy passes through the stages of growth, the need for communication facilities increases, so that spreading economic and business matters can be settled with ease. Thus in Pakistan the share of the telecom sector alone in GDP has increased to three per cent. In this sector foreign direct investment (FDI) has exceeded two arab dollars.</p><p>From this sector there has also been an increase in the income of the government. Thus in 2009-10 the government obtained 357 billion (arab) rupees from here in the form of taxes and duties, whereas in 2001-02 only ten arab rupees had been obtained. Further, because of the progress of this sector employment opportunities have increased; thus in 2010 in this sector more than ten lakh people got employment directly and indirectly.</p><p>At this time in Pakistan there are seven companies providing mobile phone service (among which Mobilink (Mobilink), Telenor (Telenor) and Warid (Warid) are big companies). Thus because of severe competition among them consumers are getting more facilities at low prices, because of which in 2000 only three lakh individuals had mobile phones, whereas in 2011 their number increased to ten crore twenty lakh.</p><p>The authority of fixed-line service was earlier with only one institution, i.e. Pakistan Telecommunication Limited (PTCL), but in 2003 its monopoly was ended. The responsibility of the deregulation (Deregulation) policy of the telecom sector is now with the Pakistan Telecom Authority (PTA). In spite of deregulation, at this time the government holds sixty-two per cent of the shares of PTCL. At this time within the country, for the convenience of the public, there are three and a half lakh public call offices (PCOs).</p>",
