   "content": "<p>2. According to the decision of the Rural Banking Advisory Committee, banks are being established in small villages and mandis.</p><p>3. For the development of trade and industry, abundant loan facilities have been given to business people.</p><p>4. The habit of saving among the public has been promoted.</p><p>5. To encourage investment in the government and private sectors, the rate of interest was reduced on such loans as are issued for fixed industrial investment.</p><p>6. Concession in the rate of interest was also made on loans given for fixed agricultural investment.</p><p>7. Under the Export Finance Scheme the rate of interest of loans given on behalf of banks was also reduced.</p><p>8. The rate of interest of the loan given to banks for the export of locally prepared machinery has been reduced.</p><p>9. Pakistani banks have played an important role in collecting savings from rural and urban areas and giving loans to the needy. The government has taken many measures in connection with the Islamic economic system in the country. Thus effort is being made to establish an interest-free economy. Moreover the banks of Pakistan have also played that role for economic development which banks usually play and which has been mentioned in the previous pages.</p><p>10. In July 1991 the “Commission for moulding the economy into the Islamic system” (Commission for Islamisation of the Economy) was established, so that it may propose such measures with whose help the Islamic economic system may be established.</p><p>11. In 1994-95 the Pakistan Banking Council issued a Qarz-e-Hasana scheme, under which Qarz-e-Hasana is provided to poor but intelligent students for higher education.</p><p>12. For reducing and economising the unnecessary expenses of banks, and for giving a better rate of compensation to depositors (Depositors), banks introduced Golden Hand Shake Schemes (Golden Hand Shake Schemes) in 1997-98. Therefore under them 20,803 employees were relieved by January 1998. Further, 265 branches going in loss were also closed.</p><p>State Bank of Pakistan (STATE BANK OF PAKISTAN)</p><p>The State Bank of Pakistan is the central bank of our country. It was established on 1 July 1948. Its inauguration was done by Quaid-e-Azam Muhammad Ali Jinnah. On 30 June 2012 this bank completed 64 years of its life. During this time it has played a prominent role in restoring the ruined economic conditions of the country and putting it on the path of economic development. This is the most important financial institution of Pakistan, and it fulfils all those responsibilities which are entrusted to a central bank. (It is also called “Bank Daulat-e-Pakistan”.)</p><p>Constitution and management of the bank</p><p>The total paid-up capital of the State Bank is ten crore rupees, which consists of shares of one hundred rupees each. Before coming into national custody in 1947, 51 per cent of its total shares (capital) were the property of the central government and the remaining 49 per cent the property of the public. Its management is entrusted to the Central Board of Directors and Local Board of Directors. The former consists of the following: one governor and chairman, and six members (Members). The appointment of the governor … (continued on p.199)</p>",
