   "content": "<p>The general reasons for supply becoming less are as follows:</p><p>☆ shortage of the factors of production, e.g. lack of efficient workers, artisans, mechanics, technicians, required raw material and capable organisers etc.;</p><p>☆ shortage of capital goods, machines, spare parts and other development equipment;</p><p>☆ decrease in supply within the country because of the export of domestic goods increasing to remove the shortage of foreign exchange;</p><p>☆ decrease in the import of necessary goods because of the shortage of foreign exchange (Foreign Exchange), or for the sake of giving protection (Protection) to the infant industries (Infant Industries) of the country from external competition (i.e. because of putting heavy tax on foreign imports);</p><p>☆ decrease in agricultural production because of unfavourable weather, e.g. drought or excessive rains, floods, natural calamities and plant diseases.</p><p>Besides these, these are the reasons for the increase in the demand for goods (and the rising of prices):</p><p>(a) increase in the consumption expenditure (Consumption Expenditure) of ordinary people;</p><p>(b) increase in investment expenditure (Investment Expenditure);</p><p>(c) increase in the supply of money;</p><p>(d) increase in the disposable income (Disposable Income) of people because of reduction in taxes;</p><p>(e) increase in external demand for national exports;</p><p>(f) increase in salaries and wages on the demand of government officials and non-government workers and employees.</p><p>Kinds of inflation (Kinds of Inflation)</p><p>Inflation can be of two kinds: first, because of increase in demand, or “demand-pulled inflation” (Demand Pull Inflation); and second, because of increase in costs, or “cost-pushed inflation” (Cost Push Inflation).</p><p>By demand-pulled inflation is meant that if the demand of consumers or employers or the government for goods and services increases, which becomes more than the quantity and supply of goods, then increase in prices, i.e. inflation, will appear.</p><p>By cost-pushed inflation this is meant, that the costs of producing goods, especially the wages of labourers, increase, and because of them prices rise; then inflation will begin.</p><p>However, after research and survey experts have drawn this conclusion, that in countries in which low-grade inflation is prevalent, there the process of economic development is strong and stable. Contrary to it, in countries in which inflation or deflation exists at a high rate, there the pace of economic development is extremely slow. Therefore gradually rising prices create very suitable and favourable conditions for healthy economic development. Thus fast-moving and severe inflation should be stopped.</p><p>[The English quotation attributed to “PAS” is summarised:] a predictable, slowly rising price level gives the most favourable setting for healthy economic growth. (PAS)</p><p>Remedies of inflation (Remedies of Inflation)</p><p>The best remedy of inflation is this, that the supply of goods be increased and demand be reduced; for this purpose the following measures should be … (continued on p.212)</p>",
