   "content": "<p>16. Write the nisab of zakat.</p><p>A. If a person has 7½ tola of gold, 52½ tola of silver, and cash money equal to 200 dirhams, and he keeps it with himself for one year, then 2.5% zakat will be imposed on it. On minerals 20%. On cattle — camels 5, cows 30, sheep and goats 40 — if there are this many, then zakat will apply on them. On trade goods too zakat will be imposed at the rate of 2.5%.</p><p>17. Is zakat an Islamic tax?</p><p>A. No! Zakat and tax are separate concepts. Zakat is a pillar of Islam; its nisab is a fixed limit which has been fixed in Islam. There is no exemption from zakat, whereas tax is the income of the government; the government imposes it and also collects it; its rate keeps changing; for some reasons tax can also be exempted.</p><p>18. When was zakat enforced in Pakistan?</p><p>A. In Pakistan the enforcement of zakat took place on 20 June 1980 on the day of Friday, and the year of zakat began from 1 Ramadan 1400 AH.</p><p>19. When was ushr enforced in Pakistan?</p><p>A. During 1982-83 the government announced that from the coming rabi crop ushr is compulsorily enforced, which will be 5% of the produce of canal-irrigated land.</p><p>Section II (Section II)</p><p>Question 2: Write detailed answers to the following questions.</p><p>1. State the sources of income of the central government of Pakistan.</p><p>2. Take a review of the tax system of Pakistan.</p><p>3. Present proposals for making the revenue system of Pakistan better.</p><p>4. State the big objectives of the fiscal policy (Fiscal Policy) of the central government.</p><p>5. Discuss the sources of income of the provincial governments of Pakistan. (Mirpur Azad Kashmir Board 2010, Multan Board 2011)</p><p>6. Who are the recipients of zakat?</p><p>7. Write a note on the economic and social importance of zakat. (Bahawalpur Board 2011)</p><p>Question 3: More than one answer has been given for each question; of them put the mark (✓) on the correct answer.</p><p>1. In Pakistan the financial year begins:</p><p>(a) from 1 January  (b) from 1 April</p><p>(c) from 1 July ✓  (d) from 1 October</p><p>2. In Pakistan central excise is imposed on these:</p><p>(a) imports  (b) exports</p><p>(c) industrial production ✓  (d) agricultural production</p><p>3. This is included in the sources of income of the provincial government:</p><p>(a) income tax  (b) property tax ✓</p><p>(c) sales tax  (d) gift tax</p>",
