   "content": "<p>(continued from p.253) … it is evident that in Pakistan the pace of industrial development has become fast.</p><p>2. Petrol and products (Petroleum &amp; Products)</p><p>With regard to value, in the imports of Pakistan petroleum and its manufactures are at number one in importance; because in 1969-70, 20 crore rupees were spent on their import, and in 2001-02, 17,257 crore rupees, whereas in the nine months of 2010-11, 69,239 crore rupees were spent. The reason for the expenditure on this head increasing is that in the international market an increase has occurred in the price of petrol.</p><p>3. Transport equipment (Transport Equipment)</p><p>In Pakistan engines, coaches, wagons and other vehicles are imported in considerable quantity, so that communications in the country progress, because economic development too is linked with it. Thus compared with 1969-70, in 1990-91 their import became 32 times. In the nine months of 2010-11 equipment of 12,388 crore rupees was imported.</p><p>4. Chemicals (Chemicals)</p><p>Chemicals too play a prominent role in industrial development, and their import too has kept increasing. In 1969-70 chemicals of only 9 crore 10 lakh rupees were imported, but in the nine months of 2010-11, of 28,423 crore rupees.</p><p>5. Food items (Food Products)</p><p>Pakistan is an agricultural country, but in spite of this it is not self-sufficient in food items. Thus it has to import grain and pulses, and the import of these goods has kept increasing continuously; e.g. in 1969-70 an amount of five and a half crore rupees was spent on the import of grain, pulses and flour, whereas during 2010-11 (July–March), 3,378 crore rupees were spent on this head. Another worrying thing is that the import expenditure on tea is increasing; e.g. in 1971-72 sixteen crore rupees and during 2010-11, 1,690 crore rupees. Therefore its consumption should be reduced. From the import of these things a heavy burden falls on our reserves of foreign exchange, because if Pakistan becomes self-sufficient in food items, then this same amount can be spent on the import of capital goods (Capital Goods), from which much increase can occur in industrial development.</p><p>6. Iron, steel (Steel) and manufactures</p><p>An increase is occurring in the import of these goods; e.g. in 1951-52 iron, steel and its manufactures of sixteen crore sixty lakh rupees and metals of one crore sixty lakh rupees were imported, whereas in the nine months of 2009-10 iron, steel and their manufactures of 10,073 crore rupees were imported.</p><p>7. Edible oils (Edible Oils)</p><p>In Pakistan the import of edible oils too has kept increasing; e.g. in 1975-76 these were imported for 104 crore rupees, whereas in the nine months of 2010-11 for 12,285 crore rupees.</p><p>8. Chemical fertilisers (Chemical Fertilizers)</p><p>The expenditure on their import too is increasing. Although by it an increase occurs in the productive capacity of the agricultural sector, effort should be made that its production within the country be increased.</p><p>9. Electrical goods (Electrical Goods)</p><p>Considerable expenditure occurs on the import of electrical goods too. In 1969-70 goods of only 21 crore rupees were imported, whereas in … (continued on p.255)</p>",
