   "content": "<p>(continued from p.261) … (29 to 31 December 1988) and the twelfth session (2 to 6 January 2004) were held in Islamabad (Pakistan). In its fourteenth session (held in New Delhi, April 2007) these countries were included as observers: China, Japan, America, the European Union and Iran.</p><p>Important objectives of the organisation</p><p>The basic objectives of SAARC (the organisation) are these:</p><p>i) promoting the fields of science and technical sciences for economic and social development by means of mutual cooperation; increasing regional trade; cooperating in matters of common interest; and jointly striving for development together with international aid organisations.</p><p>In the fourteenth conference of SAARC (held April 2007) the following important decisions were taken:</p><p>(1) India gave permission for duty-free imports for the less developed countries of SAARC; further, it relaxed visa restrictions for students, teachers and journalists.</p><p>(2) A SAARC University will be established whose campuses will be in all member countries.</p><p>(3) The atmosphere of real peace and security in South Asia will be promoted, because security and development have a deep mutual relationship.</p><p>(4) All kinds of obstacles to regional trade will be removed.</p><p>(5) Effort will be made that for future generations this whole region consisting of these countries be made a region of peace, development and prosperity, and for it determination, wisdom and insight will have to be demonstrated.</p><p>(6) A gas pipeline of Iran and India will be laid by means of Pakistan.</p><p>(7) The production of drugs (i.e. narcotics) in Afghanistan is a danger to regional peace, because the money obtained from the drug business is being used for terrorism; therefore its production will be stopped.</p><p>(8) Emphasis was put on this, that ease be created in the production of goods and services of member countries, in the movement of the public, and in the exchange of knowledge, capital, culture and ideas.</p><p>(9) A “SAARC village” will be established in member countries.</p><p>(10) An independent South Asian Commission for the Alleviation of Poverty will be established.</p><p>By implementation of the decisions of the SAARC conference there is a possibility that after many (geographical and political) disputes are resolved, these countries too, like the twenty-five (25) countries of the European Union, will adopt the status of one political and economic unit. Thus these fields of Pakistan and other member countries will develop: mutual trade, industry, transport, politics, culture and civilisation, scientific and technical education and research, mutual cooperation and political agreement etc. However, in this connection it is necessary that the Kashmir dispute between Pakistan and India be resolved according to the principles of justice and basic human rights.</p><p>World Trade Organisation (World Trade Organization (WTO))</p><p>The World Trade Organisation (WTO) was established in 1995. This is an international multilateral (Multilateral) organisation. Its head office (Headquarters) is in the Swiss city of Geneva. This organisation, in connection with trade taking place at the world level, makes laws and rules, and further removes the differences and disputes (Disputes) arising between member countries. The members of this organisation have basically … (continued on p.263)</p>",
