   "content": "<p>(continued from p.290) … in which high moral values get promotion at the individual and social level; human sympathy and goodwill are fully observed; in earning and spending wealth a distinction is maintained between halal and haram and permissible and impermissible; simplicity, economy and self-sacrifice are kept in view; and human dignity, self-respect and honour and esteem are cared for. The support of the needs and necessities of the weak, the needy, the disabled, orphans, widows, travellers, the sick, the unemployed and the old is declared a collective responsibility. On the other side, in the capitalist system the foundation of all economic struggle is only the “profit motive”, and in it the moral welfare, wellbeing and spiritual purity of man is not considered necessary to be cared for; rather in this system these evils are commonly seen: exploitation of the weak, concentration of wealth and income, wide class division and luxurious living alongside starvation, groups of people lost in extravagance and waste, miserliness and money-hoarding, and hoarding of grain and goods and black-marketing etc. In the socialist system the concept of private ownership does not exist; this too is against Islam, and keeping all people forcibly and compulsorily at one standard is against nature. In short, the economic system of Islam is better in every respect than capitalism and socialism. In this connection the difference becomes clearly evident from the study of the chosen features of the different systems, which have been entered in the concerned chapters. May Allah Most High grant us the ability to establish the Islamic system in Pakistan. Amen!</p>",
